  "historical_analysis": "Early developments in partitioning and clustering established standard k-means and basic local convergence guarantees, but revealed fundamental vulnerabilities to arbitrary outliers and noise (MacQueen, 1967; Cuesta-Albertos et al., 1997). This motivated the introduction of trimmed k-means, exploration tools based on variance functionals, and robust adaptations designed to handle contamination (García-Escudero et al., 2003; Georgogiannis, 2016). More recently, research has branched into modern computational efficiency guarantees in metric spaces (Grunau & Rozhoň, 2022; Agrawal et al., 2023), specialized updates for edge and distributed learning (Al Saleh & Azar, 2026), and non-parametric probabilistic formulations designed to replace heuristic trimming quotas (Iannaccio & Vichi, 2026).",
  "research_gaps": [
    {
